        
        {/* Section 02 */}
        <section class="os-card" style="background: #ffffff; border: 1px solid var(--border-medium); padding: 40px;">
          <h2 id="overused-term" class="os-h2" style="margin-bottom: 20px; color: var(--ink-black);">
            The Word Everyone's Using and Almost Nobody's Defining
          </h2>
          <p style="margin-bottom: 16px;">
            "AI agent" might be the most overused, under-defined phrase in business software right now. Every product launch in the last eighteen months seems to claim it. Half of them are chatbots with a new coat of paint. The other half are genuinely something new — software that doesn't just answer your question, it goes and does the thing.
          </p>
          <p style="margin-bottom: 16px;">
            That gap matters if you're a business trying to figure out where to invest. Buy the wrong kind of "agent" and you end up with an expensive chat window that summarizes things you already knew. Buy — or build — the right kind, and you get something that quietly runs research, drafts outreach, triages your inbox, audits your website, and flags the deals about to fall through, on a schedule, without asking, every single day.
          </p>
          <p style="margin: 0;">
            This guide draws that line clearly: what an AI agent is, what separates it from the assistants and chatbots most people are used to, the architecture underneath a working agent, where businesses are genuinely getting value from them, what it costs, and where agents can go wrong.
          </p>
        </section>

        {/* Section 03 */}
        <section class="os-card" style="background: #ffffff; border: 1px solid var(--border-medium); padding: 40px;">
          <h2 id="what-is-an-agent" class="os-h2" style="margin-bottom: 20px; color: var(--ink-black);">
            What an AI Agent Actually Is
          </h2>
          <p style="margin-bottom: 24px;">
            An AI agent is a software system that can <strong>perceive its environment</strong>, <strong>reason about what it's perceiving</strong>, <strong>decide on a course of action</strong>, and then <strong>take that action</strong> — autonomously, or with minimal human direction — in pursuit of a defined goal.
          </p>

          <div style="display: grid; grid-template-columns: repeat(auto-fit, minmax(240px, 1fr)); gap: 16px; margin-bottom: 24px;">
            <div style="padding: 20px; background: var(--bg-cream-alt); border: 1px solid var(--border-light); border-radius: var(--radius-md);">
              <div style="font-weight: 800; font-size: 16px; color: var(--ink-black); margin-bottom: 6px;">1. Perceive</div>
              <p style="font-size: 0.95rem; margin: 0; line-height: 1.5;">The agent takes in information — a webpage, an inbox, a database, a calendar, a CRM record — not just a single prompt typed by a human.</p>
            </div>
            <div style="padding: 20px; background: var(--bg-cream-alt); border: 1px solid var(--border-light); border-radius: var(--radius-md);">
              <div style="font-weight: 800; font-size: 16px; color: var(--ink-black); margin-bottom: 6px;">2. Reason</div>
              <p style="font-size: 0.95rem; margin: 0; line-height: 1.5;">It interprets what it's perceiving. Not just "summarize this," but "given this situation, what's the right next move?"</p>
            </div>
            <div style="padding: 20px; background: var(--bg-cream-alt); border: 1px solid var(--border-light); border-radius: var(--radius-md);">
              <div style="font-weight: 800; font-size: 16px; color: var(--ink-black); margin-bottom: 6px;">3. Decide</div>
              <p style="font-size: 0.95rem; margin: 0; line-height: 1.5;">It chooses a course of action from among multiple possibilities, based on a goal, not a rigid hardcoded script.</p>
            </div>
            <div style="padding: 20px; background: var(--bg-cream-alt); border: 1px solid var(--border-light); border-radius: var(--radius-md);">
              <div style="font-weight: 800; font-size: 16px; color: var(--candy-pink-hover); margin-bottom: 6px;">4. Act</div>
              <p style="font-size: 0.95rem; margin: 0; line-height: 1.5;">It does something in the world: sends an email, updates a record, flags a ticket, publishes a draft.</p>
            </div>
          </div>

          <p style="margin: 0;">
            That last piece — <strong>act</strong> — is what separates a true agent from almost everything people currently call "AI" in a business context. A chatbot that answers questions is perceiving and reasoning, sort of, but it isn't deciding or acting on anything beyond the conversation. It's advisory — a very smart mirror reflecting an answer. An agent closes that loop. It doesn't just tell you what the customer said in the ticket, it drafts the reply, checks it against your refund policy, and either sends it or queues it for approval.
          </p>
        </section>

        {/* Section 04 */}
        <section class="os-card" style="background: #ffffff; border: 1px solid var(--border-medium); padding: 40px;">
          <h2 id="agents-vs-assistants" class="os-h2" style="margin-bottom: 20px; color: var(--ink-black);">
            Agents vs. Assistants: The Distinction That Actually Matters
          </h2>
          <p style="margin-bottom: 24px;">
            This is the single most common point of confusion in this space, and it's the exact split a well-designed AI platform should make explicit rather than blur.
          </p>

          <div style="display: grid; grid-template-columns: 1fr 1fr; gap: 20px; margin-bottom: 24px;">
            <div style="background: var(--bg-cream-alt); border: 1px solid var(--border-medium); border-radius: var(--radius-md); padding: 24px;">
              <span class="os-badge" style="background: #ffffff; border: 1px solid var(--border-medium); margin-bottom: 10px;">Chat-Driven Specialist</span>
              <h3 style="font-size: 18px; font-weight: 700; color: var(--ink-black); margin-bottom: 10px;">AI Assistant (Copilot)</h3>
              <ul style="list-style: none; display: flex; flex-direction: column; gap: 8px; font-size: 0.95rem;">
                <li>• <strong>Trigger:</strong> Reacts only when a human types a prompt.</li>
                <li>• <strong>Execution:</strong> Human in the loop for every single step.</li>
                <li>• <strong>Tools:</strong> Internal context memory and conversational responses.</li>
                <li>• <strong>Output:</strong> Temporary chat window dialogue.</li>
                <li>• <strong>Analogy:</strong> A specialist sitting across the desk waiting for your next question.</li>
              </ul>
            </div>

            <div style="background: #ffffff; border: 2px solid var(--candy-pink); border-radius: var(--radius-md); padding: 24px; box-shadow: var(--shadow-sm);">
              <span class="os-badge os-badge-pink" style="margin-bottom: 10px;">Autonomous Worker</span>
              <h3 style="font-size: 18px; font-weight: 700; color: var(--ink-black); margin-bottom: 10px;">AI Agent (Worker)</h3>
              <ul style="list-style: none; display: flex; flex-direction: column; gap: 8px; font-size: 0.95rem;">
                <li>• <strong>Trigger:</strong> Runs on a schedule or event trigger autonomously.</li>
                <li>• <strong>Execution:</strong> Multi-step autonomous planning with approval gates.</li>
                <li>• <strong>Tools:</strong> Live web scrapers, WHOIS, SQL databases, CRMs, APIs.</li>
                <li>• <strong>Output:</strong> Persistent workspace, database writes, queued drafts.</li>
                <li>• <strong>Analogy:</strong> A specialist who arrived early, completed the groundwork, and queued decisions.</li>
              </ul>
            </div>
          </div>

          <p style="margin: 0;">
            Neither replaces the other. The strongest AI deployments use both deliberately: assistants for anything that benefits from human thinking alongside the AI in real time (strategy, nuance, judgment calls), and agents for recurring, well-defined, rules-governed work that just needs human review on the final decisions.
          </p>
        </section>

        {/* Section 05 */}
        <section class="os-card" style="background: #ffffff; border: 1px solid var(--border-medium); padding: 40px;">
          <h2 id="autonomous-vs-supervised" class="os-h2" style="margin-bottom: 20px; color: var(--ink-black);">
            Why "Autonomous" Doesn't Mean "Unsupervised"
          </h2>
          <p style="margin-bottom: 16px;">
            There's a persistent myth that an AI agent is defined by full autonomy — fire it off and it does whatever it wants, unchecked. That's not autonomy, that's risk with extra steps, and no serious business should want it, nor does any credible agent platform work that way.
          </p>
          <div style="background: var(--bg-cream-alt); border-left: 4px solid var(--status-green); padding: 18px 24px; border-radius: 6px; margin-bottom: 20px;">
            <strong style="color: var(--status-green); font-size: 1.05rem;">The Approval-Gated Autonomy Pattern:</strong>
            <p style="margin: 6px 0 0; font-size: 0.98rem; line-height: 1.6;">
              The agent handles the entire mechanical pipeline on its own (researching a prospect, drafting personalized outreach, checking against messaging guidelines) right up to the point where an action leaves the platform. At that boundary, it stops and queues the action for a human to review, approve, edit, or reject.
            </p>
          </div>
          <p style="margin: 0;">
            A well-built agent does ninety percent of a task that's genuinely mechanical — research, first draft, data pull, pattern check — and leaves the ten percent requiring actual judgment sitting in front of a human, clearly labeled, ready for a decision. That's not a limitation. That's the point.
          </p>
        </section>

        {/* Section 06 */}
        <section class="os-card" style="background: #ffffff; border: 1px solid var(--border-medium); padding: 40px;">
          <h2 id="under-the-hood" class="os-h2" style="margin-bottom: 20px; color: var(--ink-black);">
            What's Actually Happening Under the Hood
          </h2>
          <p style="margin-bottom: 24px;">
            A handful of core architectural components make an agent function in production:
          </p>

          <div style="display: grid; grid-template-columns: repeat(auto-fit, minmax(300px, 1fr)); gap: 16px;">
            <div style="padding: 18px; background: var(--bg-cream-alt); border: 1px solid var(--border-light); border-radius: 8px;">
              <strong style="color: var(--ink-black);">1. Goal or Objective:</strong> Clearly defined purpose (e.g. "Audit this website every 6 hours and flag SEO regressions").
            </div>
            <div style="padding: 18px; background: var(--bg-cream-alt); border: 1px solid var(--border-light); border-radius: 8px;">
              <strong style="color: var(--ink-black);">2. Perception & Input:</strong> Live crawling, inbox ingestion, CRM webhook listeners, and telemetry feeds.
            </div>
            <div style="padding: 18px; background: var(--bg-cream-alt); border: 1px solid var(--border-light); border-radius: 8px;">
              <strong style="color: var(--ink-black);">3. Persistent Memory:</strong> Context across sessions and previous audits so it doesn't repeat work.
            </div>
            <div style="padding: 18px; background: var(--bg-cream-alt); border: 1px solid var(--border-light); border-radius: 8px;">
              <strong style="color: var(--ink-black);">4. Reasoning & Planning:</strong> Deconstructs raw observations into actionable decisions and priorities.
            </div>
            <div style="padding: 18px; background: var(--bg-cream-alt); border: 1px solid var(--border-light); border-radius: 8px;">
              <strong style="color: var(--ink-black);">5. Real Tool Use (MCP):</strong> Headless scrapers, WHOIS, SQL execution, and external API invocation.
            </div>
            <div style="padding: 18px; background: var(--bg-cream-alt); border: 1px solid var(--border-light); border-radius: 8px;">
              <strong style="color: var(--ink-black);">6. Action & Output:</strong> Persistent workspaces, queue management, and human approval checkpoints.
            </div>
            <div style="padding: 18px; background: var(--bg-cream-alt); border: 1px solid var(--border-light); border-radius: 8px; grid-column: 1 / -1;">
              <strong style="color: var(--ink-black);">7. Feedback Cadence:</strong> Continuous execution on cron schedules (every 15m, 6h, or daily).
            </div>
          </div>
        </section>

