        {/* Section 09 */}
        <section id="common-mistakes" class="os-card" style="background: #ffffff; border: 1px solid var(--border-medium); padding: 40px;">
          <h2 class="os-h2" style="margin-bottom: 20px; color: var(--ink-black);">
            Common Mistakes Businesses Make When Adopting AI Agents
          </h2>
          <div style="display: flex; flex-direction: column; gap: 14px;">
            <div style="padding: 16px; background: #fff1f2; border: 1px solid #fecdd3; border-radius: 8px; color: #9f1239;">
              <strong>1. Automating an ill-defined process:</strong> If 3 people do a task 3 different ways, an agent just automates the mess. Standardize SOPs first.
            </div>
            <div style="padding: 16px; background: #fff1f2; border: 1px solid #fecdd3; border-radius: 8px; color: #9f1239;">
              <strong>2. Skipping shadow-mode:</strong> Failing to run 2 weeks of supervised outputs before enabling external actions.
            </div>
            <div style="padding: 16px; background: #fff1f2; border: 1px solid #fecdd3; border-radius: 8px; color: #9f1239;">
              <strong>3. Measuring output volume instead of outcome quality:</strong> 50 low-reply drafts are worse than 12 high-converting personalized ones.
            </div>
            <div style="padding: 16px; background: #fff1f2; border: 1px solid #fecdd3; border-radius: 8px; color: #9f1239;">
              <strong>4. Under-investing in review:</strong> Assuming the AI "has it handled" without dedicating time to the approval queue.
            </div>
            <div style="padding: 16px; background: #fff1f2; border: 1px solid #fecdd3; border-radius: 8px; color: #9f1239;">
              <strong>5. Opaque team communication:</strong> Rolling out agents silently without explaining roles, oversight, and expectations to existing staff.
            </div>
          </div>
        </section>

        {/* Section 10 */}
        <section class="os-card" style="background: #ffffff; border: 1px solid var(--border-medium); padding: 40px;">
          <h2 id="multi-agent" class="os-h2" style="margin-bottom: 20px; color: var(--ink-black);">
            Multi-Agent Collaboration: When One Agent Isn't Enough
          </h2>
          <p style="margin-bottom: 16px;">
            Real business outcomes rarely live inside a single department. A board deck needs sales data, a finance model, a headcount update, and a strategic narrative tying it together.
          </p>
          <p style="margin: 0;">
            Multi-agent collaboration coordinates specialized domain agents that automatically hand off work to each other with shared context memory. A single generalist agent asked to do everything is mediocre; narrow specialists that collaborate cleanly outperform it every single time.
          </p>
        </section>

        {/* Section 11 */}
        <section class="os-card" style="background: #ffffff; border: 1px solid var(--border-medium); padding: 40px;">
          <h2 id="what-it-costs" class="os-h2" style="margin-bottom: 20px; color: var(--ink-black);">
            What This Actually Costs & Real ROI
          </h2>
          <p style="margin-bottom: 16px;">
            Platform-based approaches have compressed agent setup from quarters to days. Ongoing costs include platform subscription, model token consumption, and human review time.
          </p>
          <div style="background: var(--bg-cream-alt); padding: 20px; border-radius: 8px; border: 1px solid var(--border-medium);">
            <strong style="color: var(--ink-black);">The Real ROI Formula:</strong>
            <p style="margin: 4px 0 0;">
              <code>Net ROI = (Previous Cost of Task) - (Platform + Inference Cost + Human Review Time)</code>
            </p>
          </div>
        </section>

        {/* Section 12 */}
        <section class="os-card" style="background: #ffffff; border: 1px solid var(--border-medium); padding: 40px;">
          <h2 id="honest-risks" class="os-h2" style="margin-bottom: 20px; color: var(--ink-black);">
            The Honest Risks & Security Considerations
          </h2>
          <ul style="list-style: none; display: flex; flex-direction: column; gap: 12px; font-size: 0.98rem;">
            <li>• <strong>Hallucination:</strong> Acting on plausible but false facts makes approval gates essential.</li>
            <li>• <strong>Over-scoping:</strong> Unchecked expansion of agent permissions without matching oversight.</li>
            <li>• <strong>Tool & Data Sprawl:</strong> Always enforce least-privilege tool permissions.</li>
            <li>• <strong>Audit Gaps:</strong> Ensure immutable, reviewable logs for every tool call and state change.</li>
          </ul>
        </section>

        {/* Section 13 */}
        <section class="os-card" style="background: #ffffff; border: 1px solid var(--border-medium); padding: 40px;">
          <h2 id="deployment-framework" class="os-h2" style="margin-bottom: 20px; color: var(--ink-black);">
            A Practical Framework for Safe Deployment
          </h2>
          <div style="display: grid; grid-template-columns: repeat(auto-fit, minmax(260px, 1fr)); gap: 14px;">
            <div style="padding: 16px; background: var(--bg-cream-alt); border-radius: 8px;">1. Start with recurring, well-defined manual tasks.</div>
            <div style="padding: 16px; background: var(--bg-cream-alt); border-radius: 8px;">2. Insist on approval gates for outside writes.</div>
            <div style="padding: 16px; background: var(--bg-cream-alt); border-radius: 8px;">3. Run in shadow mode for 2+ weeks first.</div>
            <div style="padding: 16px; background: var(--bg-cream-alt); border-radius: 8px;">4. Set a defined cron cadence, not unmonitored free-running.</div>
            <div style="padding: 16px; background: var(--bg-cream-alt); border-radius: 8px;">5. Log everything and ensure logs are auditable.</div>
            <div style="padding: 16px; background: var(--bg-cream-alt); border-radius: 8px;">6. Match access strictly to the job (least-privilege).</div>
            <div style="padding: 16px; background: var(--bg-cream-alt); border-radius: 8px;">7. Assign a named human owner to every agent.</div>
            <div style="padding: 16px; background: var(--bg-cream-alt); border-radius: 8px;">8. Review scope and performance quarterly.</div>
          </div>
        </section>

        {/* Section 14 & 15 */}
        <section class="os-card" style="background: #ffffff; border: 1px solid var(--border-medium); padding: 40px;">
          <h2 id="evaluating-platforms" class="os-h2" style="margin-bottom: 20px; color: var(--ink-black);">
            What to Look for in an AI Agent Platform
          </h2>
          <p style="margin-bottom: 16px;">
            Evaluate platforms on 6 strict criteria: real approval queues, specific tool-use surfaces (MCP), persistent cross-run memory, exportable audit trails, explicit separation between assistants and agents, and verified zero-retention data privacy.
          </p>
        </section>

        <section class="os-card" style="background: #ffffff; border: 1px solid var(--border-medium); padding: 40px;">
          <h2 id="real-vs-marketing" class="os-h2" style="margin-bottom: 20px; color: var(--ink-black);">
            How to Tell a Real Agent from Marketing
          </h2>
          <p>
            Does it run on a schedule? Does it call external tools? Does it produce persistent workspace outputs? Does it have human approval gates? Can you inspect its execution log? If yes, it's a real agent. If it only replies in a chat window when you ask, it's an assistant.
          </p>
        </section>

        <section class="os-card" style="background: #ffffff; border: 1px solid var(--border-medium); padding: 40px;">
          <h2 id="future" class="os-h2" style="margin-bottom: 20px; color: var(--ink-black);">
            Where This Is Headed in 2026 and Beyond
          </h2>
          <p>
            The line between assistants and agents will keep sharpening. Multi-agent coordination will become standard, Model Context Protocol (MCP) will dominate tool surfaces, and approval-gated autonomy will remain the gold standard for enterprise adoption.
          </p>
        </section>

